### B. Burning-number algorithm verdict

The transition used in `can_burn(k)` is:


$$B \leftarrow B \cup N(B) \cup \{v\}$$


where $v \in V \setminus B$.

1. **Timing and Mechanics:**
At step $t$, fire propagates from the set $B_{t-1}$ of vertices already burning at step $t-1$ to its open neighborhood $N(B_{t-1})$. Concurrently, an unburned vertex $v \in V \setminus B_{t-1}$ is selected as the step-$t$ source. The newly burned set becomes $B_t = B_{t-1} \cup N(B_{t-1}) \cup \{v\}$. In step $t+1$, fire spreads from $B_t$, causing $v$ to spread fire to $N(v)$ starting at step $t+1$. This directly mirrors the standard definition of graph burning.
2. **Redundant Source Selection:**
A chosen source $v \in V \setminus B_{t-1}$ may lie inside $N(B_{t-1}) \setminus B_{t-1}$. In this scenario, $v$ would have burned during step $t$ via propagation anyway, making the source selection "redundant" ($B_{t-1} \cup N(B_{t-1}) \cup \{v\} = B_{t-1} \cup N(B_{t-1})$). Allowing $v \in N(B_{t-1}) \setminus B_{t-1}$ expands the search tree with suboptimal or equivalent branches, but it cannot produce false positives or omit valid non-redundant burning sequences. Thus, the condition $v \in V \setminus B_{t-1}$ preserves exactness.
3. **State Space Memorylessness:**
The set $B_t$ of currently burning vertices fully determines the propagation set $N(B_t)$ at step $t+1$. The arrival order of vertices into $B_t$ has no bearing on future fire spread. Therefore, memoizing states by `(step, B)` in DFS is mathematically exact and sound.

